  <Section id="why" bg="secondary" border>
    <SectionHeader
      eyebrow="Why"
      title="Rust underneath, machine authorship on top"
      description="Why a new language, rather than a new tool for an old one. A design intent with an argument behind it, not a result yet."
    />
    <div class="grid-3">
      <Card className="flow">
        <h3>Rust is the platform</h3>
        <p>
          Mzizi is designed to lower to Rust, the way TypeScript compiles to
          JavaScript, and Mzizi Roots is its component model, the way React is
          JavaScript's. Its surface has no borrows, lifetimes or ownership
          (RFC-0001 §1.8): the compiler is meant to carry that plumbing so an agent
          never writes it. The aim is that Mzizi does Rust better. That is the goal
          Phase 0 is measured on, not a result. Today only a service lowers, to a
          local Rust + axum package; no component lowers yet.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card className="flow">
        <h3>A different bottleneck</h3>
        <p>
          No language in wide use today, TypeScript, Python, C++ and Rust included,
          was designed for the reader that now writes much of its code. They assume a
          human is typing: reading docs, holding context in their head, tolerating a
          few seconds per compile. Mzizi starts from an agent iterating against a
          compiler thousands of times, where compile speed, the information density
          of an error message, and how many tokens a codebase costs to read are
          first-order metrics rather than nice-to-haves.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card className="flow">
        <h3>The machines that need the help most</h3>
        <p>
          It designs for small open-weight models with limited parameters, context
          and long-range attention, which is why the benchmark's headline model is a
          ~7B one. It is a design intent with an argument behind it. It is not a
          result yet.
        </p>
      </Card>
    </div>
  </Section>

